pub fn purpose -> String =
  "Use a Set when the question is whether a value belongs, and repeated equal values should appear once. Set<Int> is a collection of distinct integers. Use Array or List when repeated occurrences and positions are part of the data."

pub fn order -> String =
  "Members can be read in insertion order. Repeated insertion keeps the first position; removal followed by insertion moves the member to the end. Set equality compares membership, not that order. Eq<A> and Hash<A> in signatures specify compatible equality and hashing; standard Int already supplies them."

pub fn example -> String =
  "Start with members 3, 1 and 2. Ensure that 1 is present, add a new 4 in another result, and retain the original. The returned Set is the update: the old value does not change."

pub fn reading -> String =
  "import names std/set as sets and std/json as json. let binds a name. [] is an Array, and sets.fromIterable collects its distinct values. Calls use spaces between arguments and parentheses around nested calls. sets.toArray reads insertion order; json.encodeString formats it as JSON. pub effect fn main and do execute the prints in order, and println adds a newline."

pub fn outcomes -> String =
  "Inserting 1 keeps [3,1,2]. Inserting 4 returns [3,1,2,4]. original remains [3,1,2]. The final example inserts 0 into an empty Set. <Int> explicitly gives that empty Set its element type; a surrounding annotation could provide it instead. () is Unit, not an element to store."

pub fn comparison -> String =
  "TypeScript Set already provides membership, uniqueness and insertion order. add/delete mutate its receiver, so copying new Set(original) first preserves the original when the task requires it. Seseragi insert/remove return a new Set. The insert and remove pages show executable counterparts with identical small-integer inputs and output."

pub fn combining -> String =
  "union and intersection take right first, then left. They preserve left order; union appends right-only members. difference removed values excludes members named by the first input. isSubsetOf superset values checks every member of the second input. Read each page’s directional examples before swapping arguments."

pub fn limits -> String =
  "fromIterable fully reads finite inputs; endless input does not finish. sets.map may reduce the member count when different inputs become equal results. Converting with toArray or toList preserves insertion order. A Set is not automatically sorted."
